indications_limitations:
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;p&gt;B. &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Nationally Covered Indications&lt;&sol;p&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;  

&lt;p&gt;ESA treatment for the anemia secondary to myelosuppressive anticancer chemotherapy in solid tumors, multiple myeloma, lymphoma, and lymphocytic leukemia is only reasonable and necessary under the following specified conditions:&lt;&sol;p&gt;  

&lt;ul&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  The hemoglobin level immediately prior to initiation or maintenance of ESA treatment is &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL (or the hematocrit is &amp;lt; 30%).  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  The starting dose for ESA treatment is the recommended FDA label starting dose, no more than 150 U&sol;kg&sol;3 times weekly for epoetin and 2.25 mcg&sol;kg&sol;1 time weekly for darbepoetin alpha. Equivalent doses may be given over other approved time periods.  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Maintenance of ESA therapy is the starting dose if the hemoglobin level remains below 10g&sol;dL (or hematocrit is &amp;lt; 30%) 4 weeks after initiation of therapy and the rise in hemoglobin is &amp;ge; 1g&sol;dL (hematocrit &amp;ge; 3%);  &lt;&sol;li&gt;   
&lt;li&gt;  For patients whose hemoglobin rises &amp;lt; 1g&sol;dl (hematocrit rise &amp;lt; 3%) compared to pretreatment baseline over 4 weeks of treatment and whose hemoglobin level remains &amp;lt; 10g&sol;dL after the 4 weeks of treatment (or the hematocrit is &amp;lt; 30%), the recommended FDA label starting dose may be increased once by 25%. Continued use of the drug is not reasonable and necessary if the hemoglobin rises &amp;lt; 1g&sol;dl (hematocrit rise &amp;lt; 3%) compared to pretreatment baseline by 8 weeks of treatment.  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Continued administration of the drug is not reasonable and necessary if there is a rapid rise in hemoglobin &amp;gt; 1g&sol;dl (hematocrit &amp;gt; 3%) over 2 weeks of treatment unless the hemoglobin remains below or subsequently falls to &amp;lt; 10g&sol;dL (or the hematocrit is &amp;lt; 30%). Continuation and reinstitution of ESA therapy must include a dose reduction of 25% from the previously administered dose.  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  ESA treatment duration for each course of chemotherapy includes the 8 weeks following the final dose of myelosuppressive chemotherapy in a chemotherapy regimen.  &lt;&sol;li&gt;&lt;&sol;ul&gt;  

&lt;strong&gt;&lt;p&gt;C. &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Nationally Non-Covered Indications&lt;&sol;p&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;  

&lt;p&gt;ESA treatment is not reasonable and necessary for beneficiaries with certain clinical conditions, either because of a deleterious effect of the ESA on their underlying disease or because the underlying disease increases their risk of adverse effects related to ESA use. These conditions include:&lt;&sol;p&gt;  

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Any anemia in cancer or cancer treatment patients due to folate deficiency, B-12 deficiency, iron deficiency, hemolysis, bleeding, or bone marrow fibrosis;  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  The anemia associated with the treatment of acute and chronic myelogenous leukemias (CML, AML), or erythroid cancers;  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  The anemia of cancer not related to cancer treatment;  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Any anemia associated only with radiotherapy;  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Prophylactic use to prevent chemotherapy-induced anemia;  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Prophylactic use to reduce tumor hypoxia;  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Patients with erythropoietin-type resistance due to neutralizing antibodies; and  &lt;&sol;li&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Anemia due to cancer treatment if patients have uncontrolled hypertension.  &lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;  

&lt;strong&gt;&lt;p&gt;D. &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Other&lt;&sol;p&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;  

&lt;p&gt;Local Medicare Administrative Contractors may continue to make reasonable and necessary determinations on all other uses of ESAs not specified in this National Coverage Determination.&lt;&sol;p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;See the &lt;a href=&quot;https:&sol;&sol;www.cms.gov&sol;Regulations-and-Guidance&sol;Guidance&sol;Manuals&sol;Internet-Only-Manuals-IOMs-Items&sol;CMS012673&quot;&gt;Medicare Benefit Policy Manual&lt;&sol;a&gt;, chapter 11, section 90 and chapter 15, section 50.5.2 for coverage of ESAs for end-stage renal disease-related anemia.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

